<RunSettings>
  <!-- The Nightly tier (#703): [Explicit] + [Category("Nightly")] fixtures — chaos/stress/torture workloads kept
       out of the PR gate for their duration but genuinely RUN here.

       NumberOfTestWorkers=1 for the same reason the gate shards use it: the suite carries process-static state
       (ArchetypeRegistry, profiler rings, epoch registry) and these fixtures deliberately saturate the box, so a
       parallel run would measure interference rather than the engine.

       DefaultTimeout is 180 s rather than workers1.runsettings' 10 s because these workloads legitimately run for
       seconds (OlcBTreeRaceStressTests defaults to a 30 s wall).

       DO NOT rely on DefaultTimeout to bound a HANG. Measured on this branch (2026-08-07) against the
       ChaosStressTests livelock (#695): NUnit could not terminate it. .NET has no Thread.Abort, so a CPU-bound
       spin ignores the per-test deadline entirely — the run had to be killed from outside. DefaultTimeout bounds
       a test that CRAWLS, not one that spins.

       The real guard is vstest's `--blame-hang`, which kills the test host and names the hanging test in a
       Sequence_*.xml. nightly-suppressed.yml passes it for exactly that reason; without it a single livelock
       consumes the whole job timeout and reports nothing about which test caused it. -->
  <NUnit>
    <NumberOfTestWorkers>1</NumberOfTestWorkers>
    <DefaultTimeout>180000</DefaultTimeout>
  </NUnit>
</RunSettings>
